          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong vertical transformation
            is an analogue of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            which goes between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+functors"} double functors
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
            %strong horizontal transformation.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are strict double categories regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G\colon C\to D
            are double functors regarded as internal functors in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            (there being two ways).
          %p
            More explicitly, a vertical transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\colon F\to G
            consists of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For every object
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                              %mi c
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi α
                                    %mi c
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi G
                              %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ F c \\ \downarrow ^{\alpha_c} \\ G c}
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                For every horizontal arrow
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon c_1 \to c_2
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 1
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %mi F
                                  %mi p
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi α
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi c
                                        %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo ⇓
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi α
                                    %mi p
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi α
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi c
                                        %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi G
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 1
                            %mtd
                              %munder
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %mi G
                                  %mi p
                            %mtd
                              %mi G
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{F c_1 &amp; \overset{F p}{\to} &amp; F c_2\\
                      ^{\alpha_{c_1}}\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow^{\alpha_p}&amp; \downarrow^{\alpha_{c_2}}\\
                      G c_1&amp; \underset{G p}{\to} &amp; G c_2}
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                For each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi c
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_c\colon c\to c
                is its horizontal identity, then the square
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{1_c}
                is equal to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi α
                              %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{\alpha_c}
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_c
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon c_1\to c_2
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mn 2
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\colon c_2\to c_3
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_p
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_q
                is equal to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mrow
                            %mi q
                            %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{q p}
          %p The notion of horizontal transformation is dual.
          %h2#the_double_category_of_double_functors The double category of double functors
          %p
            Another characterization of transformations between double categories comes from observing that the 1-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DblCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblCat
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^C
            are double functors, its vertical arrows are vertical transformations, and its horizontal arrows are horizontal transformations. Its squares are a sort of “square modification” relating a pair of vertical and a pair of horizontal transformations.
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            It is easy to modify the explicit definition to handle the cases when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are weak in one direction or the other, and/or when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            are pseudo functors in one direction or the other, by composing with appropriate coherence constraints. In this way, we obtain many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
            of double categories.
          %p
            It is also easy to define
            %em vertical
            transformations between double functors which are
            %em horizontally
            lax or colax, and dually. In fact, given double categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C,D,C',D'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C\to D
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'\colon C'\to D'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon C\to C'
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G'\colon D\to D'
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi F
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %mi G
                              %mo ′
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi F
                              %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                          %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{C &amp; \overset{F}{\to} &amp; D \\
                  ^G\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow&amp; \downarrow^{G'}\\
                  C'&amp; \underset{F'}{\to} &amp; D'}
          %p
            despite the fact that the composites
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G' \circ F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'\circ G
            do not exist as double functors of any sort. Such transformations are the squares of a large double category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Dbl
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dbl
            whose objects are double categories, whose horizontal arrows are lax functors, and whose vertical arrows are colax functors. This, in turn, is a special case of a construction which works for algebras over any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
          %p
            Finally, we can also define vertical transformations between functors of (horizontally)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+categories"} virtual double categories
